---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Benchmark from '../components/Benchmark.astro';
import study from '../data/study.json';
import '../styles/evidence.css';

const signed = (v: number) => `${v > 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(v * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const pct = (v: number) => `${(v * 100).toFixed(0)}%`;
const farthest = study.horizon_skill.at(-1)!, longBand = study.bands.at(-1)!, trend = study.trend.universes.project.scenarios.base;
---
<Layout title="Forecasts meet reality" description="A public scoreboard for AI crypto forecasts. Every Bitcoin, Ethereum and Solana prediction is locked, then scored against reality — misses included.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> A PUBLIC RESEARCH NOTEBOOK</p>
        <h1>Forecasts<br /> meet <em>reality.</em></h1>
        <p class="hero-intro">Every hour an AI model predicts where Bitcoin, Ethereum and Solana will trade 24 and 72 hours later. We lock each prediction, wait for the outcome and publish the score — the misses too.</p>
        <p class="hero-body">A research experiment built on TimesFM, Google’s forecasting model, with news evaluation alongside. Not investment advice.</p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/forecasts/">See the latest forecasts <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#evidence">Explore the evidence <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a></div>
        <p class="hero-caption"><a href="https://github.com/moinsen-dev/crypto-oracle" rel="external noreferrer">Explore the source on GitHub ↗</a> · Code, tests and the research method.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero-notebook" aria-labelledby="first-result">
        <div class="notebook-top"><span>FIELD NOTE / 001</span><span>17 SEP 2026</span></div>
        <div class="notebook-title"><span class="outlined-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↘</span><h2 id="first-result">The simple<br /> baseline won.</h2></div>
        <p>In the first historical experiment, guessing “the price stays the same” produced less error than the model.</p>
        <div class="finding-rows">
          <div><span class="coin-mark">B</span><span>Bitcoin <small>BTC</small></span><strong>+5.2%<small>more error than last price</small></strong></div>
          <div><span class="coin-mark">E</span><span>Ethereum <small>ETH</small></span><strong>+6.1%<small>more error than last price</small></strong></div>
          <div><span class="coin-mark">S</span><span>Solana <small>SOL</small></span><strong>+9.3%<small>more error than last price</small></strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="notebook-bottom"><span class="mini-square" aria-hidden="true"></span><p>24 hours ahead · 365 daily forecasts per coin · backtest<br /> <b>No forecasting advantage demonstrated.</b></p></div>
      </aside>
    </section>
    <section class="live-now" aria-labelledby="live-title" data-live-now>
      <div class="live-head"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>LOCKED RIGHT NOW</p><h2 id="live-title">What the model has committed to.</h2><p class="section-caption" data-live-note>The newest 24-hour forecasts, frozen in the public journal before their deadline. Each one is scored when that deadline passes, whatever happens.</p></div>
      <div class="live-cards" data-live-cards><p class="live-fallback">The newest locked forecasts are listed in the <a href="/forecasts/">forecast journal</a>.</p></div>
    </section>
    <div class="facts-strip" aria-label="Experiment facts">
      <div><strong>3</strong><span>assets under observation<br /> <b>BTC · ETH · SOL</b></span></div>
      <div><strong>24 / 72<span>h</span></strong><span>forecast horizons<br /> <b>Issued before evaluation</b></span></div>
      <div><strong>365</strong><span>daily historical origins<br /> <b>Per asset and horizon</b></span></div>
    </div>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="question">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE QUESTION</p><h2 id="question">Can context make<br /> a forecast more useful?</h2><p>A convincing story is easy. A useful forecast has to survive comparison, data problems and the passage of time.</p></div>
      <div class="three-columns">
        <article class="principle"><span class="index">01 / OBSERVE</span><h3>Start with the series.</h3><p>TimesFM reads 512 completed hourly closing prices. Its forecasts are stored with the model revision, inputs and issue time.</p><span class="tag">Implemented</span></article>
        <article class="principle"><span class="index">02 / UNDERSTAND</span><h3>Put news in context.</h3><p>FinBERT and Laya classify headlines. We preserve when each item became available. Laya annotations do not change a price forecast.</p><span class="tag">Collecting evidence</span></article>
        <article class="principle"><span class="index">03 / CHALLENGE</span><h3>Let reality answer.</h3><p>Compare forecasts with the actual outcome and simple baselines. A separate news experiment can progress when enough prospective data exists.</p><span class="tag tag-outline">Benefit still unproven</span></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div id="evidence" class="anchor-section"><Benchmark /></div>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="follow-ups">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE EVIDENCE / 002–008</p><h2 id="follow-ups">Then we asked<br /> the follow-up questions.</h2><p>Shorter horizons, the uncertainty bands, slower signals, and then the news, the state of the market and other coins. Fixed rules, every variant reported, including the ones that went nowhere.</p></div>
      <div class="evidence-teaser">
        <a href="/evidence/#horizons"><span class="index">002 / HORIZONS</span><strong>{signed(farthest.skill)}</strong><p>No horizon rescues the model. From one hour to three days, the last price stays the better forecast.</p><span class="text-link">Read the note <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a>
        <a href="/evidence/#bands"><span class="index">003 / UNCERTAINTY</span><strong>{signed(1 - longBand.volatility_band.interval_score / longBand.timesfm_raw.interval_score)}</strong><p>How far a price may move is forecastable. Four volatility numbers beat the model’s own band.</p><span class="text-link">Read the note <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a>
        <a href="/evidence/#trend"><span class="index">004 / TREND</span><strong>{pct(trend.rebalanced.max_drawdown)} → {pct(trend.trend_filter.max_drawdown)}</strong><p>One idea survived its costs: a slow trend filter halved the deepest loss. It did not earn more.</p><span class="text-link">Read the note <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="evidence-more">Four more places we looked, 005–008: headlines, market signals and other coins. None of them says which way the price goes. <a class="text-link" href="/evidence/#headlines">Read what we found <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="section architecture-preview" aria-labelledby="behind-scenes">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE SCENES</p><h2 id="behind-scenes">A small system.<br /> <em>A visible paper trail.</em></h2><p>Models run on a dedicated research server. The public site delivers a curated, dated snapshot. Browsing this page does not contact the models or the private dashboard.</p><a class="text-link" href="/method/">Walk through the architecture <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="pipeline-preview" aria-label="Research data flow">
        <div><span>01</span><strong>Market data & news</strong><small>Source + observation time</small></div><div class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div><span>02</span><strong>Forecasts & annotations</strong><small>Pinned inputs + model identity</small></div><div class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div><span>03</span><strong>Journal & evaluation</strong><small>Stored prediction → later outcome</small></div><div class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div class="pipeline-public"><span>04</span><strong>This public notebook</strong><small>Selected aggregate results only</small></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="paper-teaser" aria-labelledby="paper-title">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">THE LIVE PAPER EXPERIMENT</p><h2 id="paper-title">$10,000.<br /> Entirely virtual.</h2><p>The virtual portfolios record decisions, simulated trades and changing balances, with costs, data checks and a reason for every action. The newest one follows the one rule that survived our tests: a slow trend filter, against a rebalanced benchmark.</p><a class="button button-light" href="/paper-portfolio/">Follow the portfolios <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="paper-stamp"><span class="pill pill-light">VIRTUAL · OBSERVING LIVE</span><p>Starting capital</p><strong>$10,000<span>USD</span></strong><div class="paper-line"></div><p>Recorded decisions.<br /> Transparent simulated results.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="closing-note"><p class="eyebrow">BUILT TO BE QUESTIONED</p><h2>The useful part is what we learn.</h2><p>Follow the decisions, the checks and the things that did not work as hoped.</p><a class="text-link" href="/build-notes/">Read the development journal <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </div>
</Layout>
<script is:inline src="/scripts/home.js" defer></script>
